  "why": "The statement is explicitly a family ('a number of closely related conjectures'), so nothing here is whole. Results proving isoparametricity for closed minimal constant-scalar-curvature hypersurfaces under an extra constancy hypothesis (constant H_3, A_3, K, f_3, Dupin) restrict the class of objects within the conjecture's own setting and are scored case rather than variant. Second-gap constant improvements (Chen, Cheng-Li-Wei, Willmore approximate bound) are bound. Firester-Tsiamis refutes Chern's original general-codimension discreteness question, a named member of the family, so it is a case with refuted direction and counterexample; Li-Zhao's flat tori likewise refute Lu's listed variant for surfaces in codimension >= 3. Higher-codimension pinching results under different hypotheses (flat normal bundle, max S gap, Lei) are variant. Cheng-Li 2024 is digested via journal publication. The only AI disclosure (Li-Zhao 2608.16095) says AI provided 'better inequality estimates and improve constants', which is mathematics in the result, so assisted; that event is a variant regardless.",
  "doubts": "Whether Firester-Tsiamis and Li-Zhao flat tori bear on the bullet depends on counting the Wikipedia article's general-codimension and Lu formulations as part of 'closely related conjectures'; the January 2024 article text was not checked. Treating added-constancy-hypothesis results as case rather than variant is a close call. The 2608.16095 AI quote comes from a model-rendered fetch and the declaration otherwise describes AI as auxiliary; assisted vs none is close.",
